INT. GRAIN SILO - BASE LEVEL - NIGHT

Rust-streaked concrete. A single hanging bulb swings. Wheat dust hangs in the beam. Somewhere above, metal ticks.

DELPHINE CROSBY, thirties, holds a flashlight steady. MAXWELL "MAX" ODUYA, twenties, kneels by a hatch with a crowbar. OLD ENRICO FABBRI, seventies, sits on an upturned bucket. A STRANGER stands motionless at the foot of the ladder, face in shadow.

DELPHINE
Everyone. Stay where you are.

Nobody moves. The ticking stops.

MAX
(to himself)
It's the pipes. It's just the pipes.

ENRICO
(to Max)
Boy. Put the bar down.

Max doesn't. The Stranger takes one slow step into the light. Wheat dust clings to the shoulders of his coat. His eyes do not blink.

STRANGER
I've been up there a long time.

DELPHINE
(to the Stranger)
Who are you?

STRANGER
Anselm. My name was Anselm.

The bulb dies. In the dark: the ladder groans. Delphine's flashlight snaps back on.

ANSELM is now six feet from Max, who has raised the crowbar. The wheat around his boots is moving.

ANSELM
It remembers me.

ENRICO
(whisper)
Don't let him touch the grain.

DELPHINE
(to the group)
Back to the door. All of you. Now.

Max swings. Anselm catches the bar in one hand. The silo shudders, and the floor of wheat begins to rise.
